@page "/not-found"

@* Rendered two ways, which is why it is a component and not markup inlined in Routes.razor:
   - client-side, as the <NotFound> fragment of the router (a link to a URL with no page); and
   - server-side, as the re-execution target of app.UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute("/not-found")
     in the host's Program.cs. That second path matters because a deep link that matches no page
     never reaches Blazor at all - ASP.NET routing answers it with a bare 404 before
     MapRazorComponents is consulted - so on a first visit (no service worker yet) the router's
     fragment would never run.
   It lives in the client project because Routes.razor binds the router to that assembly only. *@

@* Not in DocsCatalog, so the copy lives here. noindex comes from SiteMetadata.NoIndexUrls - and
   is what keeps a soft-404 (this page rendered under a URL a crawler followed) out of the index. *@
<PageOutlet Slug="not-found"
            Title="Not found - bit Bswup"
            Description="There is no page at this address. Browse the bit Bswup documentation from here." />

<div class="docs-not-found">
    <h1>404</h1>
    <p role="alert">Sorry, there's nothing at this address.</p>
    @* Two ways out rather than one: back to the front door, or straight into the
       page most people who mistype a docs URL were actually looking for. *@
    <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-primary button-large" href="/">
            <Icon Name="arrow-left" />
            Back to the docs
        </a>
        <a class="button-secondary button-large" href="getting-started">
            <Icon Name="book" />
            Getting Started
        </a>
    </div>
</div>
